comments.body:
@participant9 Comment https://github.com/golang/go/issues/54265#issuecomment-1268708470 includes:

> One other detail that has become clear recently is that when we say Go 1.20 is required, it will be the latest point release of Go 1.20 at the time we adopt the requirement, so that any bugs fixed in the first few point releases don't have to be worked around.

Should this be moved to Go1.23 milestone with early-in-cycle label to do that, or a separate issue, to make it easier not to forget?

@participant8 as another concrete example of what @participant1 is mentioning, consider https://go-review.googlesource.com/c/go/+/508135

This CL forwards some `sort` package functions to much-faster `slices` sorts (*), but we can't do it unless the compiler actually supports generics and the `slices` package. For now the CL works around it with build tags, but we'd really like to be able to clean it up eventually (namely when the bootstrap version is bumped to 1.21) -- otherwise the Go toolchain will be full of build-tag-gated code and will become increasingly difficult to maintain.

(*) This can "magically" speed up many Go programs once this CL is part of a release

> I don't understand why these builds are "cumbersome". Building Go 1.4 -> Go 1.17 -> Go 1.20 -> Go 1.22 an extra iteration of a shell script loop. Adding a few more bytes to the shell script once a year to extend the list of versions to iterate over also does not seem "cumbersome".

@reporter it requires us to ensure that all intermediate versions build on all architectures (riscv64, s390x, ppc64le, i386 …) at all times (with newer musl libc versions etc). In your bootstrap path, for example, Go 1.4 doesn't support riscv64 (which is also why we bootstrap with gccgo presently). This is by no means an academic issue, on these more obscure architectures Go does break occasionally with musl (see #51787 or #58385 for example). The overhead involved with the maintenance of these intermediate versions is what makes this more cumbersome than a simple bootstrap path like gcc → gccgo → Go 1.20.

That being said, I do understand why it is desirable for Go compiler developers to be able to use the new and shiny features provided by newer Go versions, but this certainly makes it more difficult to have a clean bootstrap path if gccgo isn't updated accordingly.
